        "id": 9443,
        "name": "Julia",
        "username": "Julia",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/j/2bfe46/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2014-02-07T09:51:38.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Has 'Sentinels' taken over your everyday vocabulary ?</p><p>I used say 'flip' when I meant 'becoming really angry', as in \"Good thing he left, one more minute and I would have flipped.\"</p><p>A while ago, I realised &nbsp;that I was starting to use it more in a sense that Villain Cards flip, which in most cases means that they become really unpredictable/crazy/hard to deal with/harder to get rid of. Which, I guess, <em>sounds</em> exactly the same as the other 'flip' but , hey, <em>&nbsp;I</em> know that there is a difference.</p><p>(I have had other games which found their way in my everyday vocabulary, for example, I still call all negative effects 'Bad Stuff' - a relic from when I still played a lot of Munchkin.)</p><p>So, have you ever encountered your boss on Advance mode? 'End of Day'ed an entire project? Referred to someone who was off sick as 'Incapped'?</p><p>And more importantly, can you think of any more ways in which Sentinels can 'improve' the English language (or at least make it a damn sight more colourful)?</p>",
        "post_number": 1,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T01:40:14.418Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
        "reply_to_post_number": null,
